<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chp-conditional-flow" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Conditional Flow</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs step by step in a straight line. Real programs
			need to react to data or user input, choosing different actions when conditions change.
		</p>

		<p>
			For example, suppose <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> stores the amount of money on an Amazon
			gift card and <pf>total_due</pf> stores the price of a purchase. Before completing the
			purchase, your program needs to ask a yes-or-no question:
			<q>Is <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> greater than or equal to <pf>total_due</pf>?</q>
		</p>

		<p>
			Questions like this are called <term>logical expressions</term>, and their answers are
			called <term>logical values</term> (i.e., <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>). Two types of
			structures that let you ask questions and make decisions based on their answers are
			<pf>if</pf>-statements and <pf>switch</pf>-statements. These structures allow you to
			control the flow of your program, executing different blocks of code depending on the
			conditions you specify.
		</p>

		<p>
			In this section, we will learn how MATLAB evaluates logical expressions and how to use <pf>if</pf> and <pf>switch</pf> statements to create programs that can make decisions and respond to different situations.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			In the previous chapter, <pf>if</pf>-statements gave your code the ability to choose between different paths based on conditions. Now we introduce a different kind of flow control: <term>repetition</term>. Loops let you repeat a block of code multiple times, either a known number of times or until some condition changes. Think of replacing dozens of copy-pasted lines with just a few representative lines.
		</p>

		<p>
			MATLAB provides two primary looping structures:
			<dl width="narrow">
				<li><title>for-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat once for each value in a known list or range. Use these when you know how many iterations you need before the loop starts.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li><title>while-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat as long as a condition remains true. Use these when the number of iterations depends on something that changes during execution.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			The chapter also introduces three commands: <pf>continue</pf>, <pf>break</pf>, and <pf>return</pf>, which let you skip iterations, exit loops early, or exit functions immediately when the answer is already known.
		</p>

		<p>
			By the end of this chapter, you will be able to automate repetitive calculations, accumulate running totals and products, search for values that satisfy conditions, and approximate mathematical quantities to any desired accuracy.
		</p>

	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-if-statement.ptx" /> 
	<xi:include href="sec-switch-statement.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<p>
			<objectives xml:id="takeaways-conditional-flow">
				<ul marker="square">
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if</term>.
							If <pf>condition</pf> is true, run <pf>myCode</pf>:
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condition
									myCode
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if-else</term>.
							If <pf>condition</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>, otherwise run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condition
									myCodeA
								else
									myCodeB
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if-elseif-else</term>.
							If <pf>conditionA</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>, otherwise if <pf>conditionB</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
							If none are true, run <pf>myCodeC</pf>.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if conditionA
									myCodeA
								elseif conditionB
									myCodeB
								else
									myCodeC
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>switch</term>.
							Compares <pf>value</pf> against a list of exact values.
							If <pf>value</pf> is <pf>valueA</pf>, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>. If <pf>value</pf> is <pf>valueB</pf>, run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
							If no values match, <pf>myCodeC</pf> runs.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								switch value
									case valueA
										myCodeA
									case valueB
										myCodeB
									otherwise
										myCodeC
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Choosing between <pf>switch</pf> and <pf>if</pf>-<pf>elseif</pf></term>.
							Use <pf>switch</pf> when you are comparing one variable against a small set of exact values (especially strings or integers).
							Use <pf>if</pf>-<pf>elseif</pf> when your conditions involve ranges, inequalities, or multiple variables.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</objectives>
		</p>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>